\section{Blueprint Visualization and Video Export Module}

\subsection{Overview}
We introduce a real-time blueprint visualization subsystem designed for
interactive schematic rendering, telemetry display, and automated video
capture. The module provides a deterministic 60\,FPS rendering loop,
isometric wireframe geometry, animated scan-line diagnostics, and
browser-native WebM export. This subsystem serves as a front-end
visualization layer for autonomous simulation engines and technical
design interfaces.

\subsection{System Architecture}
The renderer is implemented using the HTML5 Canvas API with a
JavaScript-based animation kernel. The visualization surface is a
1280\,$\times$\,720 canvas enclosed within a styled container that
emulates a neon blueprint aesthetic. The module consists of:

\begin{itemize}
    \item A grid-based blueprint background with cyan vector lines.
    \item Telemetry overlays including system designer attribution,
          frame rate, resolution, and ISO timestamp.
    \item Concentric radar rings and rotating wireframe geometry.
    \item A dynamic horizontal scan-line with positional readout.
    \item A deterministic animation loop driven by
          \texttt{requestAnimationFrame}.
    \item A 60\,FPS video capture pipeline using
          \texttt{canvas.captureStream()} and \texttt{MediaRecorder}.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Rendering Kernel}
The rendering loop updates two state variables: a rotation angle
$\theta(t)$ and a scan-line position $y_{\mathrm{scan}}(t)$:
\begin{align}
    \theta(t) &= \theta_0 + 0.015\,t, \\
    y_{\mathrm{scan}}(t) &= (3t) \bmod 720.
\end{align}
Each frame consists of:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Clearing the canvas and drawing the blueprint grid.
    \item Rendering telemetry text in cyan monospace type.
    \item Drawing radar rings and rotating wireframe squares.
    \item Rendering the animated scan-line and its positional label.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Video Capture and Export}
The module supports automated recording and download of a 5-second
WebM video. The capture pipeline is initialized via:
\begin{verbatim}
const stream = canvas.captureStream(60);
const mediaRecorder = new MediaRecorder(stream, {
    mimeType: 'video/webm;codecs=vp9'
});
\end{verbatim}
Captured chunks are assembled into a Blob and downloaded as
\texttt{blueprint\_schematic\_render.webm}. The recording process is
fully autonomous and requires no external dependencies.

\subsection{User Interface}
The interface includes a neon-styled control button and a status
indicator. The button triggers recording, disables itself during
capture, and re-enables upon completion. The status indicator provides
real-time feedback on rendering and export operations.

